==================================================================
CRITERION 7 — warningsKey is a function of CONTENT, not of order or
              identity.                                            *** FAIL ***
==================================================================
The implementation (internal/handlers/accounts/handlers.go, warningsKey) writes
each warning into an FNV-64a hash IN SLICE ORDER with a NUL delimiter and never
sorts. It is order-SENSITIVE. The worker's own doc comment on the function says
so verbatim -- "a cheap, deterministic, order-sensitive fingerprint" -- while
the pageData.WarningsKey field comment two hundred lines earlier claims it is
"a fingerprint of Warnings' content". Those two statements disagree; the
criterion requires the field comment's version.

$ go test -count=1 -run 'TestCHK_WarningsKey_OrderInvariance' -v ./internal/handlers/accounts/
  zz_checker_test.go:24: CHK order: key(a,b)="1abc0897194b44de" key(b,a)="2b5c49f725efa5b6"
  zz_checker_test.go:26: ORDER-SENSITIVE: same content in different order gives
    different keys: "1abc0897194b44de" vs "2b5c49f725efa5b6"
  --- FAIL: TestCHK_WarningsKey_OrderInvariance (0.00s)
(a and b hold the SAME TWO warning strings, only permuted.)

The other halves of the property DO hold:
$ go test -count=1 -run 'TestCHK_WarningsKey_EmptyAddRemove' -v ./internal/handlers/accounts/
  --- PASS
  warningsKey(nil) == "" and warningsKey([]string{}) == ""
  add:        k(["w1"])="5f5177194908097f"  k(["w1","w2"])="6f66aebe07bff13a"  (differ)
  remove:     k(["w2"]) != k(["w1"])                                          (differ)
  delimiter:  k(["ab","c"])="ad22872f536e4705" k(["a","bc"])="401801fc84f3ca79"(differ)
  -> the NUL separator rules out the concatenation-boundary collision.
Cross-process stability (three separate `go test` processes):
  CHKKEY=fb5ff549a8c2e799
  CHKKEY=fb5ff549a8c2e799
  CHKKEY=fb5ff549a8c2e799
  -> stable across processes; FNV-64a is unseeded, so this is real.

WHICH FAILURE MODE THIS RISKS (as asked): the UN-DISMISSAL one, not the
collision one. I probed for collisions four ways (add, remove, substitute,
delimiter injection) and found none; a NEW warning will not stay hidden. What
the implementation risks is the reverse: a permutation of an UNCHANGED warning
set yields a new key, sessionStorage's stored key no longer matches, and the
banner reappears and the live region re-announces even though nothing the user
cares about changed -- exactly the "re-announced on every load" outcome the
worker's own template comment says point 14 rules out.

MITIGATION THE WORKER DID NOT CLAIM AND NO TEST PINS: today the ordering happens
to be deterministic. csvBasenames() ends with sort.Strings(out), OverlapWarnings
(accounts.go:234) emits one warning per basename in that order, and the account
IDs inside each string come from sortedByID. So the permutation above is not
currently reachable through buildPageData. That makes this a latent defect
rather than a live bug -- but the criterion states the property, the
implementation does not hold it, and NOTHING pins the two sorts that are
accidentally protecting it. TestBuildPageData_WarningsKey_ChangesWithContent
(handlers_test.go:668) tests empty-case and content-change only; there is no
order-invariance assertion anywhere.

WHAT WOULD FIX IT: sort a copy of warnings before hashing (or hash a
sorted/canonicalised set), plus a test asserting
warningsKey([a,b]) == warningsKey([b,a]).

